eak king position.\n\nReason: The Black king on e6 is critically exposed in the center of the board. Q: h5-f7 delivers check, forcing Black's king to move to a worse square (likely d5, e5, or e7), while my queen occupies a dominant central square. This forcing move maintains initiative and keeps Black's king under pressure. After the king moves, I'll have follow-up tactics with my well-placed knight on d6 and my active queen.\n\nAnswer: Q: h5-f7"}
