\n1 |RWF0|NWF0|BWF0|    |KWF0|    |    |RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, P: c2-c4, P: c2-d3, P: c2-c3, N: d6-b5, N: d6-b7, N: d6-c4, N: d6-c8, N: d6-e8, N: d6-f5, N: d6-f7, P: g2-g4, P: g2-f3, P: g2-g3, R: h1-g1, R: h1-f1, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I'm playing as White in a complex middle-game position. Black has a powerful queen on f3 that's deep in my territory, creating serious threats. Black's king on e6 is somewhat exposed in the center of the board. I have a knight actively placed on d6, which is a strong outpost. My king on e1 hasn't castled and is potentially vulnerable. Black has more developed pieces and is applying pressure.\n\nEvaluation: Let me consider the most promising moves:\n\n1. **N: d6-f7** - This knight move attacks the Black king on e6 and could lead to a check or fork opportunities. It's a forcing move that puts immediate pressure on Black's exposed king.\n\n2. **N: d6-f5** - Attacks the queen on f3 while maintaining pressure. The queen would have to move, but this doesn't create immediate threats to the king.\n\n3. **N: d6-c4** - Maintains the knight actively but doesn't create immediate threats.\n\n4. **P: c2-d3** - Attacks the Black queen on f3, forcing it to move, but this seems passive and blocks my bishop.\n\n5. **R: h1-g1** - Defensive, preparing to deal with the queen, but reactive rather than proactive.\n\nReason: **N: d6-f7** is the strongest move. It delivers a knight fork, simultaneously attacking the Black king on e6. Since the king is in check, Black must respond to the check immediately. This forcing move disrupts Black's attack and my knight on f7 will be well-placed. Even after the king moves, I maintain initiative and can continue applying pressure with my active knight. This is far superior to passive defense against the threatening queen on f3.\n\nAnswer: N: d6-f7"}
