\n1 |RWF0|NWF0|BWF0|    |KWF0|    |    |RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a3, N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, P: c2-c4, P: c2-d3, P: c2-c3, K: e1-d1, K: e1-f2, P: f6-f7, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, R: h1-g1, R: h1-f1, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I'm playing as White in a mid-to-late game position. My most advanced piece is a pawn on f6, just one square away from promoting to a queen on f7. Black's king is on h7, relatively exposed. Black has material including rooks on a6 and h8, a knight on g8, knights on e5, a bishop on d3, and several pawns. My king is still on e1 (unmoved), suggesting I haven't castled. I have rooks on a1 and h1, knights on b1, a bishop on c1, and several pawns.\n\nEvaluation: \n1. **P: f6-f7** - This pushes my pawn to the 7th rank, one move away from promotion. This is extremely threatening as next move I could promote to a queen, which would be devastating for Black. The pawn would also give check to the Black king on h7 from f7, forcing Black to respond to the threat.\n\n2. **P: c2-c3/c4** or **P: g2-g3/g4** - These are slow pawn moves that don't address the critical tactical opportunity.\n\n3. **N: b1-c3** - Develops a knight but misses the immediate winning continuation.\n\n4. **K: e1-d1/f2** - King moves that don't capitalize on the advanced f6 pawn.\n\nReason: The pawn on f6 is my biggest asset. Advancing it to f7 gives check to the Black king on h7 (the pawn on f7 attacks g8 and e8, but more importantly, it threatens promotion next turn). Even if it doesn't give immediate check, Black will be forced to deal with the promotion threat, likely by blocking with the knight from g8 or the rook from h8. This puts enormous pressure on Black and is clearly the strongest continuation. Promoting a pawn to a queen would give me overwhelming material advantage and likely lead to a quick win.\n\nAnswer: P: f6-f7"}
