k's king on g5 is dangerously exposed in the middle of the board. The move g2-g4 delivers check and severely restricts the Black king's escape squares. After g4+, the Black king will be forced to move (likely to f6, h6, f5, or h5), and each square keeps it exposed to further attacks. This forcing move maintains the initiative and exploits Black's critical weakness - the unsafe king position. In chess, when your opponent's king is exposed, you should attack it immediately rather than making slower developmental moves.\n\nAnswer: P: g2-g4"}
